(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Computer Architecture"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Computer Architecture"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Computer Architecture: Assignments\n\nThree assignments, seven questions each (21 in all), with complete solutions at the end of each assignment. Every number in the solutions was computed with a script or measured on a real machine; where a measurement is quoted it was taken on an Intel Core i5-7300U laptop (2 cores, 4 threads; 32 KB 8-way L1 data cache with 64-byte lines) and your values will differ, though the pattern should match.\n\n# Assignment 1: Performance and Pipelining (Chapters 1 and 2)\n\n**Q1. The iron law.** A program executes 3 × 10^9 instructions with CPI 2.0 on a 3 GHz processor. (a) Its execution time [2]. (b) A new compiler reduces the instruction count by 20 percent but raises the CPI to 2.3: the new time and the speedup [4]. (c) Which of IC, CPI and clock rate does each of these affect: a better compiler, a larger cache, a deeper pipeline, a new instruction set? [4]\n\n**Q2. MIPS is misleading.** Machine A runs at 3 GHz with CPI 1.5; machine B at 2 GHz with CPI 1.0. A program needs 10^9 instructions on A and 1.2 × 10^9 on B. Find the MIPS rating and the execution time of each and say which is faster and by how much [10].\n\n**Q3. Amdahl's law.** (a) A program is 80 percent parallelisable. Find the speedup on 2, 4, 8, 16 and 1000 processors and the limit [4]. (b) What parallel fraction gives a speedup of 3 on 4 processors? [3] (c) A component used 40 percent of the time is made twice as fast: the overall speedup [3].\n\n**Q4. Amdahl versus Gustafson.** A program has a 10 percent serial portion. (a) The fixed-size speedup on 16 processors by Amdahl's law [3]. (b) The scaled speedup by Gustafson's law [3]. (c) Explain why they differ [4].\n\n**Q5. Pipeline hazards.** A five-stage pipeline (IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB) writes registers in the first half of a cycle and reads them in the second half. For\n```\nlw  x1, 0(x2)\nadd x3, x1, x4\nsub x5, x3, x6\nsw  x5, 0(x7)\n```\n(a) Identify every data hazard [3]. (b) Total cycles without forwarding [3]. (c) Total cycles with full forwarding [2]. (d) Reorder `lw x1; lw x3; add x5,x1,x3; sw x5; add x7,x1,x1` to remove the stall and give the cycles before and after [2].\n\n**Q6. Pipeline performance.** (a) Loads are 25 percent of instructions and 40 percent of loads are followed by a dependent instruction (1 stall); branches are 20 percent with a 15 percent misprediction rate and a 3-cycle penalty. Find the CPI and the speedup of the 5-stage pipeline over an unpipelined machine of CPI 1 per stage-time [5]. (b) A 10 ns unpipelined datapath is split into five 2 ns stages with 0.2 ns register overhead: the clock period and the speedup [3]. (c) For 1000 instructions the ideal cycle count [2].\n\n**Q7. Power.** Dynamic power is P = alpha C V^2 f. (a) Voltage and frequency are both reduced by 15 percent: the new power relative to the old and the saving [3]. (b) Performance falls by 15 percent: the energy for a fixed task [3]. (c) Why did clock rates stop rising around 2005 and what did designers do? [4]\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 1\n\n**Q1.** Iron law: Time = IC × CPI / f.\n(a) Step 1: IC = 3 × 10⁹, CPI = 2.0, f = 3 × 10⁹ Hz.\nStep 2: time = (3 × 10⁹ × 2.0)/(3 × 10⁹) = **2.0 s**.\n(b) Step 1: new IC = 0.8 × 3 × 10⁹ = 2.4 × 10⁹; new CPI = 2.3.\nStep 2: new time = (2.4 × 10⁹ × 2.3)/(3 × 10⁹) = 5.52/3 = **1.84 s**.\nStep 3: speedup = 2.0/1.84 = **1.087** (the 20 percent drop in instructions is largely cancelled by the higher CPI).\n(c) A better compiler affects IC (and sometimes CPI through scheduling). A larger cache affects CPI (fewer stall cycles), possibly clock rate if it lengthens the cycle. A deeper pipeline raises the clock rate but also CPI (hazards and branch penalties get worse). A new instruction set affects IC and possibly CPI and clock rate (simpler or more complex instructions).\n\n**Q2.** MIPS = f/(CPI × 10⁶); time = IC × CPI / f.\nStep 1 (A): MIPS = 3000/1.5 = **2000**; time = (10⁹ × 1.5)/(3 × 10⁹) = **0.5 s**.\nStep 2 (B): MIPS = 2000/1.0 = **2000**; time = (1.2 × 10⁹ × 1.0)/(2 × 10⁹) = **0.6 s**.\nStep 3: both have MIPS = 2000, yet A is 0.6/0.5 = **1.2 times faster**: MIPS ignores how many instructions are needed for the work. Execution time of the real program is the only reliable metric.\n\n**Q3.** Amdahl: speedup = 1/((1 - f) + f/n).\n(a) Step 1: f = 0.8, so speedup = 1/(0.2 + 0.8/n).\nStep 2: n = 2: 1/(0.2 + 0.4) = **1.667**. n = 4: 1/(0.2 + 0.2) = **2.5**. n = 8: 1/(0.2 + 0.1) = **3.333**. n = 16: 1/(0.2 + 0.05) = **4.0**. n = 1000: 1/(0.2 + 0.0008) = **4.98**.\nStep 3: limit as n → ∞: 1/0.2 = **5**.\n(b) Step 1: 1/((1 - f) + f/4) = 3, so (1 - f) + f/4 = 1/3.\nStep 2: 1 - 0.75f = 0.3333, so 0.75f = 0.6667 and f = 0.6667/0.75 = **0.889**.\n(c) Step 1: the enhanced part is fraction 0.4 sped up by 2: new time = 0.6 + 0.4/2 = 0.8 (of the old time).\nStep 2: speedup = 1/0.8 = **1.25**.\n\n**Q4.** Serial fraction s = 0.1, n = 16 processors.\n(a) Amdahl: 1/(0.1 + 0.9/16) = 1/(0.1 + 0.05625) = 1/0.15625 = **6.4**.\n(b) Gustafson: scaled speedup = n - s(n - 1) = 16 - 0.1 × 15 = 16 - 1.5 = **14.5**.\n(c) Amdahl fixes the problem size, so the serial part limits the speedup. Gustafson assumes the problem grows with the machine (a bigger problem solved in the same time), so the serial part stays a small share of the run time on the larger problem. They answer different questions: \"how much faster can I solve this problem?\" versus \"how much bigger a problem can I solve in the same time?\".\n\n**Q5.** Code: lw x1,0(x2); add x3,x1,x4; sub x5,x3,x6; sw x5,0(x7).\n(a) Read-after-write (RAW) hazards: `add` needs x1 from `lw`; `sub` needs x3 from `add`; `sw` needs x5 from `sub`. (Three dependences.)\n(b) Without forwarding, a consumer's ID must wait until the producer's WB (the register is written in the first half and read in the second half), giving two stalls per dependence.\nStep 1: base time for 4 instructions in a 5-stage pipeline = 5 + (4 - 1) = 8 cycles.\nStep 2: stalls = 3 dependences × 2 = 6.\nStep 3: total **14 cycles**.\n(c) With full forwarding only the load-use hazard remains (one stall): 8 + 1 = **9 cycles**. The ALU-to-ALU and ALU-to-store dependences are forwarded without a stall.\n(d) Original order: `lw x1; lw x3; add x5,x1,x3; sw x5; add x7,x1,x1`. The `add x5,x1,x3` uses x3 from the load immediately before it, so one stall: base 5 + 4 = 9 cycles, plus 1 stall = **10 cycles**.\nReordered: `lw x1; lw x3; add x7,x1,x1; add x5,x1,x3; sw x5`: the independent `add x7` fills the load delay slot, so there is no stall: **9 cycles**.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Step 1: load-use stalls = 0.25 (loads) × 0.4 (dependent) × 1 cycle = 0.10 cycles per instruction.\nStep 2: branch stalls = 0.20 (branches) × 0.15 (mispredicted) × 3 cycles = 0.09.\nStep 3: CPI = 1 + 0.10 + 0.09 = **1.19**.\nStep 4: speedup over the unpipelined machine = 5/1.19 = **4.2** (the ideal is 5).\n(b) Step 1: stage time = 2 ns + 0.2 ns register overhead = **2.2 ns** clock period.\nStep 2: speedup = 10 ns/2.2 ns = **4.5**.\n(c) Ideal cycle count for n = 1000 instructions on k = 5 stages: k + n - 1 = 5 + 1000 - 1 = **1004 cycles** (against 5000 unpipelined, a speedup of 4.98).\n\n**Q7.** P = αCV²f.\n(a) Step 1: V and f both scale by 0.85.\nStep 2: P_new/P_old = 0.85² × 0.85 = 0.85³ = **0.614**: power falls by 38.6 percent.\n(b) Step 1: performance falls to 0.85, so a fixed task takes 1/0.85 = 1.176 times as long.\nStep 2: energy = power × time: 0.614 × 1.176 = **0.7225**: 27.7 percent less energy for the same work.\n(c) Power density reached the limit of air cooling (about 100 W per cm²): raising the frequency raises V and f together (power about f³). Designers moved to multicore processors (more parallel work at moderate frequencies), more cache, power management (DVFS, clock and power gating) and specialised accelerators.\n\n# Assignment 2: Instruction-Level Parallelism (Chapter 3)\n\n**Q1. Predictor comparison.** A loop branch is taken three times then not taken (T T T N) repeatedly. Initial state: 1-bit predictor predicts taken; the 2-bit saturating counter starts at 2 (weakly taken; states 0, 1 predict not taken, 2, 3 taken). (a) List correct (c) and wrong (W) predictions for the first 12 outcomes for both predictors [6]. (b) Steady-state accuracy of each [2] (c) A history-based predictor with 4 history bits: what accuracy does it reach and why? [2]\n\n**Q2. Other patterns.** Accuracy of the 1-bit, 2-bit and 4-bit-history predictors for (a) a loop branch taken 9 times then not taken, repeating [4]; (b) an alternating pattern T N T N ... [4]; (c) random outcomes [2].\n\n**Q3. Misprediction cost.** A 2-wide processor has base CPI 0.5, 20 percent branches and a 15-cycle misprediction penalty. (a) CPI at 90 percent and 95 percent accuracy [4]. (b) What accuracy is needed for CPI 0.6? [3] (c) Why do deeper pipelines need better predictors? [3]\n\n**Q4. In-order versus out-of-order.** Latencies: ADD 1, MUL 3, DIV 5; one instruction can start per cycle and an instruction starts in the first cycle its sources are ready (results usable the cycle after the latency).\n```\nI1  DIV R1, R2, R3\nI2  ADD R4, R1, R5\nI3  ADD R6, R7, R8\nI4  MUL R9, R7, R8\nI5  ADD R10, R4, R6\n```\n(a) Start and finish cycle of each instruction for in-order issue and total cycles [4]. (b) Same for out-of-order issue [4]. (c) What limits the speedup if the machine could issue two per cycle? [2]\n\n**Q5. Dependences and renaming.**\n```\nI1  DIV R1, R2, R3\nI2  ADD R4, R1, R5\nI3  SUB R5, R6, R7\nI4  MUL R1, R8, R9\nI5  ADD R10, R4, R1\n```\n(a) List all RAW, WAR and WAW dependences [5]. (b) Rename the registers to remove the false dependences [3]. (c) Which instructions can then start in the first cycle? [2]\n\n**Q6. Reorder buffer and speculation.** (a) What is a precise exception and why does an out-of-order machine need a reorder buffer to provide it? [4] (b) Describe what happens to speculative instructions after a branch misprediction [3] (c) Why are stores not written to the cache until they commit? [3]\n\n**Q7. Superscalar limits.** (a) The ideal CPI of a 4-wide machine [2]. (b) Name four reasons real IPC is lower [4]. (c) On a laptop with 2 cores and 4 hardware threads a fully parallel floating-point loop ran 1.4 times faster with 2 threads and 1.7 times faster with 4: explain [4].\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 2\n\n**Q1.** Outcomes (12): T T T N T T T N T T T N.\n(a) 1-bit predictor: predicts the previous outcome (initially T).\nStep 1: outcome 1 (T): predict T, correct (c). Outcome 2 (T): predict T, c. Outcome 3 (T): predict T, c.\nStep 2: outcome 4 (N): predict T, wrong (W); the predictor now holds N.\nStep 3: outcome 5 (T): predict N, W; it now holds T. Outcome 6 (T): c. Outcome 7 (T): c.\nStep 4: outcome 8 (N): W; outcome 9 (T): W; outcome 10 (T): c; outcome 11 (T): c; outcome 12 (N): W.\n1-bit result: **c c c W W c c W W c c W**.\n2-bit saturating counter (start 2, predicts taken when the counter is 2 or 3):\nStep 1: outcomes 1 to 3 (T): counter 2 → 3 → 3 → 3, predictions T, all correct.\nStep 2: outcome 4 (N): counter is 3, predicts T, wrong; counter 3 → 2 (still predicts taken).\nStep 3: outcome 5 (T): predicts T, correct; counter back to 3. The pattern repeats.\n2-bit result: **c c c W c c c W c c c W**.\n(b) Steady state: the 1-bit predictor is wrong twice per 4 outcomes (after each N and again on the next T): accuracy **50 percent** (measured over 400 outcomes: 50.2 percent). The 2-bit predictor is wrong once per 4 (only on the N): **75 percent**.\n(c) A predictor with 4 bits of history learns the pattern T T T N completely, since each of the four 4-bit histories determines the next outcome uniquely: about **98.5 percent** over 400 outcomes (misses only while warming up).\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) A loop branch taken 9 times then not taken (10 outcomes per loop).\n1-bit: wrong at the N and at the T after it: 2 errors per 10, **80 percent** (measured 80.25 percent).\n2-bit: wrong only at the exit N: 1 error per 10, **90 percent**.\n4-bit history: the exit cannot be identified from the last 4 outcomes (the history T T T T occurs both before a T and before the N), so it behaves like a 2-bit predictor: about **89 percent**.\n(b) Alternating T N T N:\n1-bit: always predicts the previous outcome, which is always wrong: **0 percent** (measured 0.25 percent).\n2-bit: the counter oscillates around the middle and is right half the time: **50 percent**.\nHistory-based: the last outcome determines the next one: about **99 percent**.\n(c) Random outcomes: about **50 percent** for all three (a measured run: 52.8, 52.5 and 52.8 percent): nothing can be learned.\n\n**Q3.** CPI = base CPI + (branch fraction) × (1 - accuracy) × penalty.\n(a) Step 1: accuracy 90 percent: 0.5 + 0.2 × 0.10 × 15 = 0.5 + 0.30 = **0.80**.\nStep 2: accuracy 95 percent: 0.5 + 0.2 × 0.05 × 15 = 0.5 + 0.15 = **0.65**.\n(b) Step 1: set 0.5 + 0.2 × m × 15 = 0.6, where m is the misprediction rate.\nStep 2: 3m = 0.1, so m = 0.0333.\nStep 3: accuracy = 1 - 0.0333 = **96.7 percent**.\n(c) The penalty (about the pipeline depth up to branch resolution) grows with depth: each misprediction wastes more work, and wider machines have more instructions in flight to discard.\n\n**Q4.** Latencies: ADD 1, MUL 3, DIV 5. An instruction starts when its sources are ready; results are usable the cycle after they finish.\n(a) In-order issue (instructions start in program order, at most one per cycle):\nStep 1: I1 (DIV R1) starts at cycle 1 and finishes at cycle 5.\nStep 2: I2 (ADD R4 ← R1 + R5) needs R1, usable from cycle 6: starts 6, finishes 6.\nStep 3: I3 (ADD R6) is behind I2 in order: starts 7, finishes 7.\nStep 4: I4 (MUL R9) starts 8, finishes 10 (3 cycles).\nStep 5: I5 (ADD R10 ← R4 + R6) starts 9 (sources ready), finishes 9.\nTotal: last finish is cycle **10**.\n(b) Out-of-order issue (an instruction starts as soon as its sources are ready):\nStep 1: I1 starts 1, finishes 5.\nStep 2: I3 (independent) starts 2, finishes 2.\nStep 3: I4 (independent) starts 3, finishes 5.\nStep 4: I2 waits for R1 (usable at 6): starts 6, finishes 6.\nStep 5: I5 needs R4 (usable at 7) and R6: starts 7, finishes 7.\nTotal **7 cycles** (30 percent fewer).\n(c) The **critical path** of true dependences I1 → I2 → I5 = 5 + 1 + 1 = 7 cycles cannot be shortened: with two issues per cycle I3 and I4 still start early but the schedule remains 7 cycles.\n\n**Q5.** Code: I1 DIV R1,R2,R3; I2 ADD R4,R1,R5; I3 SUB R5,R6,R7; I4 MUL R1,R8,R9; I5 ADD R10,R4,R1.\n(a) **RAW** (true) dependences: I2 reads R1 written by I1; I5 reads R4 written by I2; I5 reads R1 (the latest write is I4).\n**WAR** (a later write to a register an earlier instruction reads): I3 writes R5, which I2 reads; I4 writes R1, which I2 reads.\n**WAW** (two writes to the same register): I4 and I1 both write R1.\n(b) Give every new write a new physical register: I1: R1 → P1; I2: R4 → P4 (reads P1 and R5); I3: R5 → P5 (a new register, so I2 still reads the old R5); I4: R1 → P6; I5: R10 → P10 (reads P4 and P6). Now only the true dependences remain: I1 → I2 → I5 and I4 → I5.\n(c) I1, I3 and I4 have no source that depends on an earlier result, so all three can start in the first cycle (limited only by issue width).\n\n**Q6.** (a) A **precise exception** leaves the machine in a state as if all instructions before the faulting one had completed and none after it had started. An out-of-order machine finishes instructions out of order, so it needs the **reorder buffer** to hold results until they can **commit in program order**; on an exception the instructions after the faulting one are discarded and the visible state is exactly that of the commit point.\n(b) Instructions fetched after a mispredicted branch are **squashed**: the ROB entries after the branch are flushed, the rename map is restored (from a checkpoint or by walking the ROB) and the front end is redirected to the correct target. The penalty is the time to refill the pipeline.\n(c) A store whose instruction is later squashed must never become visible: the data is held in the store queue and written to the cache only when the store commits, so memory state is always precise.\n\n**Q7.** (a) An ideal 4-wide machine completes 4 instructions per cycle: CPI = 1/4 = **0.25**.\n(b) Reasons real IPC is lower: data dependences (limited instruction-level parallelism in the program); branch mispredictions; cache misses (the window fills while a miss is outstanding); limited fetch/decode bandwidth and structural limits (ports, ROB, issue queue size, physical registers).\n(c) On a 2-core CPU with SMT the two threads on a core share its execution units, so the second hardware thread adds only the throughput of the idle units (about 20 to 40 percent), not a full core. The two physical cores plus SMT therefore give 1.7 rather than 4.\n\n# Assignment 3: Memory, Multiprocessors and GPUs (Chapters 4 to 7)\n\n**Q1. Cache address breakdown.** For 32-bit addresses give the offset, index and tag bits and the number of sets for (a) a 4 KB direct-mapped cache with 16-byte blocks, (b) a 32 KB 8-way cache with 64-byte blocks, (c) a 64 KB 2-way cache with 64-byte blocks [9]. (d) Why can a 32 KB 8-way L1 with 64-byte lines be indexed in parallel with the TLB when pages are 4 KB? [1]\n\n**Q2. Cache trace.** A 64-byte cache with 16-byte blocks sees the byte addresses 0, 64, 0, 64, 16, 80, 16, 80, 0, 16. (a) Hit or miss for each access in a direct-mapped cache, a 2-way set-associative cache (LRU) and a fully associative cache [6]. (b) Classify the misses of the direct-mapped cache into compulsory, capacity and conflict [4].\n\n**Q3. AMAT and CPI.** (a) Hit time 1 ns, miss rate 5 percent, miss penalty 100 ns: AMAT [2]. (b) Two levels: L1 hit 1 ns, miss rate 10 percent; L2 hit 10 ns, local miss rate 20 percent; memory 100 ns: AMAT and the global L2 miss rate [4]. (c) Base CPI 1.0, 30 percent loads/stores, 4 percent data miss rate, 50-cycle penalty: CPI [2]. Also include a 2 percent instruction-fetch miss rate (one fetch per instruction) [2].\n\n**Q4. Measured locality.** A program summing a 4096 × 4096 int matrix took 0.012 s row by row and 0.49 s column by column on the test machine; a pointer-chasing test gave 1.5 ns for a 32 KB working set, 6 ns for 256 KB, 14 ns for 1 MB and about 110 to 125 ns for 16 to 64 MB. (a) Explain the 20 to 40 times difference quantitatively [4]. (b) What do the latency steps tell you about the cache sizes? [3] (c) Describe loop blocking for matrix multiplication [3].\n\n**Q5. Coherence.** (a) Two cores access one block: C0 read, C1 read, C0 write, C1 read, C1 write, C0 read. Give the MESI states (C0, C1) after each access and the bus transactions [6]. (b) Core 0 reads then writes twice, then core 1 writes, then core 0 reads: states and bus transactions [2]. (c) What is false sharing? On the test machine two threads incrementing adjacent counters took 0.91 s against 0.48 s when padded apart: explain and fix [2].\n\n**Q6. Roofline.** A GPU has 10 TFLOP/s peak and 500 GB/s memory bandwidth. (a) The ridge point [2]. (b) The attainable performance of SAXPY (2 FLOP, 12 bytes per element) and of a dot product (2 FLOP, 8 bytes) [4]. (c) The minimum time to add two vectors of 10^9 floats on this GPU and on a CPU with 51.2 GB/s [4].\n\n**Q7. GPUs and multicore.** (a) How many blocks of 256 threads cover 10^6 elements, and how many warps are in a block? [3] (b) Explain branch divergence in a warp [3] (c) Why do GPUs need many more threads than cores? [2] (d) Why does a program with a 5 percent serial portion gain at most 20 times from any number of cores? [2]\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 3\n\n**Q1.** For a cache: number of sets = capacity/(block size × ways); offset bits = log₂(block size); index bits = log₂(sets); tag bits = 32 - index - offset.\n(a) 4 KB direct-mapped, 16-byte blocks: sets = 4096/(16 × 1) = 256. Offset = log₂ 16 = **4**, index = log₂ 256 = **8**, tag = 32 - 8 - 4 = **20**.\n(b) 32 KB 8-way, 64-byte blocks: sets = 32,768/(64 × 8) = 64. Offset = **6**, index = **6**, tag = 32 - 12 = **20**.\n(c) 64 KB 2-way, 64-byte blocks: sets = 65,536/(64 × 2) = 512. Offset = **6**, index = **9**, tag = 32 - 15 = **17**.\n(d) The index and offset bits together (6 + 6 = 12) lie entirely within the 12-bit page offset of a 4 KB page, which is identical in the virtual and physical address. So the cache can be indexed with the virtual address while the TLB translates the page number, and the physical tag is compared afterwards (virtually indexed, physically tagged).\n\n**Q2.** 64-byte cache, 16-byte blocks: 4 lines. Block address = address/16: the accesses 0, 64, 0, 64, 16, 80, 16, 80, 0, 16 map to blocks 0, 4, 0, 4, 1, 5, 1, 5, 0, 1.\n(a) **Direct-mapped** (4 sets, set = block mod 4): blocks 0 and 4 both map to set 0, and blocks 1 and 5 both map to set 1. Each access evicts the previous block of its pair: M M M M M M M M M M (**10 misses**).\n**2-way** (2 sets, set = block mod 2): blocks 0 and 4 are both in set 0 (two ways), blocks 1 and 5 both in set 1 (two ways). Accesses: block 0 M, 4 M, 0 H, 4 H, 1 M, 5 M, 1 H, 5 H, 0 H, 1 H: **4 misses**.\n**Fully associative** (4 lines hold all of 0, 4, 1, 5): the same sequence, **4 misses**.\n(b) The 10 direct-mapped misses: **4 compulsory** (the first access to each of blocks 0, 4, 1, 5), **0 capacity** (the four blocks used fit in the cache, and the fully associative cache misses only 4 times), and **6 conflict** misses (10 - 4 = 6).\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) AMAT = hit time + miss rate × miss penalty = 1 + 0.05 × 100 = **6 ns**.\n(b) Step 1: AMAT = L1 hit + L1 miss × (L2 hit + L2 local miss × memory) = 1 + 0.1 × (10 + 0.2 × 100).\nStep 2: = 1 + 0.1 × 30 = **4.0 ns**.\nStep 3: global L2 miss rate = L1 miss rate × L2 local miss rate = 0.1 × 0.2 = **2 percent**.\n(c) Step 1: CPI = base + (memory references per instruction) × miss rate × penalty = 1 + 0.3 × 0.04 × 50 = 1 + 0.6 = **1.6**.\nStep 2: with instruction fetch misses (1 fetch per instruction at 2 percent): 1 + (1 × 0.02 + 0.3 × 0.04) × 50 = 1 + 0.032 × 50 = **2.6**.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Step 1: Row-by-row summation reads consecutive addresses, so each 64-byte line (16 ints) is fetched once and used 16 times, and the hardware prefetcher streams ahead.\nStep 2: Column-by-column access has a stride of 4096 × 4 bytes = 16 KB: every access touches a different line (and a different page, stressing the TLB), uses only 4 of the 64 bytes fetched, and the line is evicted (the 64 MB matrix is far larger than the cache) before the next column needs it.\nStep 3: The measured ratio 0.49/0.012 = 41 is explained by the extra memory traffic (up to 16 times) and the latency of non-streaming accesses.\n(b) The latency steps follow the hierarchy: about 1.5 ns up to 32 KB (L1: 4 to 5 cycles); 3.5 ns at 64 KB (partly L2) and 6 ns at 256 KB (L2); 14 ns at 1 MB (L3); then a jump to 58 ns at 4 MB (beyond the 3 MB L3, mostly DRAM) and 110 to 125 ns (DRAM). The knees at about 32 KB, 256 KB and 3 MB match the L1, L2 and L3 sizes.\n(c) **Blocking (tiling)**: split the matrices into B × B tiles chosen so that three tiles fit in the cache (3B² × element size ≤ cache size) and compute the product tile by tile. Each tile is loaded once and reused B times, reducing memory traffic from about n³ elements to about n³/B.\n\n**Q5.** MESI states: M modified, E exclusive, S shared, I invalid.\n(a) Step 1 (C0 read): C0 fetches the block: states **(E, I)**; bus transactions so far: 1.\nStep 2 (C1 read): C1 fetches it, both become shared: **(S, S)**; total 2.\nStep 3 (C0 write): C0 upgrades with an invalidate: **(M, I)**; total 3.\nStep 4 (C1 read): C0 supplies the data and writes back, both shared: **(S, S)**; total 4.\nStep 5 (C1 write): C1 invalidates C0's copy: **(I, M)**; total 5.\nStep 6 (C0 read): C1 supplies the data, both shared: **(S, S)**; total 6.\n(b) C0 read: (E, I), 1 transaction. C0 write: the E → M upgrade is silent (no bus transaction), (M, I). C0 write again: no transaction. C1 write: invalidates C0, (I, M), transaction 2. C0 read: (S, S), transaction 3. Writes to a block held exclusively are free.\n(c) **False sharing**: logically separate variables lie in the same cache line, so a write by one core invalidates the other core's copy and the line ping-pongs although nothing is truly shared. The adjacent counters took 0.91 s against 0.48 s when padded, 1.9 times longer. Fix: pad or align each thread's data to the line size (64 or 128 bytes) so each counter has its own line.\n\n**Q6.** Roofline: attainable performance = min(peak, bandwidth × arithmetic intensity).\n(a) Ridge point = peak/bandwidth = 10 × 10¹² FLOP/s ÷ 500 × 10⁹ B/s = **20 FLOP/byte**.\n(b) SAXPY: intensity = 2 FLOP/12 bytes = 0.167 FLOP/byte; performance = 500 GB/s × 0.167 = **83 GFLOP/s** (memory bound, 0.8 percent of peak).\nDot product: intensity = 2/8 = 0.25 FLOP/byte; performance = 500 × 0.25 = **125 GFLOP/s**.\n(c) Step 1: adding two vectors of 10⁹ floats reads 2 × 4 GB and writes 4 GB: traffic = 3 × 4 × 10⁹ = 12 GB.\nStep 2: GPU time = 12 GB/500 GB/s = **24 ms**.\nStep 3: CPU time = 12 GB/51.2 GB/s = **234 ms** (both limited by memory bandwidth, not arithmetic).\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) Step 1: blocks = ⌈10⁶/256⌉ = ⌈3906.25⌉ = **3907**.\nStep 2: warps per block = 256/32 = **8**.\n(b) All 32 threads of a warp share one instruction stream. When threads take different sides of an `if`, the hardware executes both paths one after the other with the non-participating threads masked off, so the warp takes the sum of the two path times and lanes sit idle.\n(c) GPUs hide memory latency by switching between warps: while one warp waits for DRAM (hundreds of cycles) others run, which requires tens of thousands of threads in flight.\n(d) By Amdahl's law the speedup is limited by 1/0.05 = **20** as the number of cores tends to infinity, because the serial part takes 5 percent of the original time regardless of the core count.","exam":"# Computer Architecture: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Which factor does a better compiler primarily change?\nA) clock rate  B) instruction count  C) cache size  D) pipeline depth\n\n**2.** Two machines both have a 2000 MIPS rating. Then\nA) they run every program equally fast  B) they may still differ in execution time on the same program  C) both have CPI 1  D) both have the same clock rate\n\n**3.** Dynamic power in CMOS is proportional to\nA) V × f  B) V^2 × f  C) V^3  D) f only\n\n**4.** A load-use hazard in the classic five-stage pipeline with forwarding costs\nA) 0 cycles  B) 1 cycle  C) 2 cycles  D) 3 cycles\n\n**5.** A branch target buffer (BTB) stores\nA) the data of recent loads  B) the target addresses of recent branches  C) the register values  D) the page table\n\n**6.** The 2-bit saturating counter predictor changes its prediction only after\nA) one wrong outcome  B) two consecutive wrong outcomes  C) four wrong outcomes  D) it never changes\n\n**7.** In Tomasulo's algorithm results are broadcast on\nA) the memory bus  B) the program counter  C) the common data bus  D) the TLB\n\n**8.** WAW dependences are removed by\nA) forwarding  B) register renaming  C) bigger caches  D) a faster clock\n\n**9.** A VLIW processor relies on\nA) the hardware to find parallelism  B) the compiler to schedule independent operations  C) the operating system  D) speculative threads\n\n**10.** Which statement about the reorder buffer is true?\nA) it speeds up cache misses  B) instructions commit from it in program order  C) it holds only loads  D) it replaces the TLB\n\n**11.** A 64 KB direct-mapped cache with 32-byte blocks (32-bit addresses) has an index of\nA) 10 bits  B) 11 bits  C) 12 bits  D) 13 bits\n\n**12.** A fully associative cache has no\nA) capacity misses  B) conflict misses  C) compulsory misses  D) hits\n\n**13.** Increasing the block size generally\nA) always lowers the miss rate  B) reduces compulsory misses but may raise the miss penalty and conflict misses  C) removes capacity misses  D) lowers the hit time\n\n**14.** Write-through with a write buffer is used to\nA) avoid stalling the processor on stores  B) avoid cache coherence  C) speed up loads  D) reduce tag bits\n\n**15.** In MESI a write to a line in the S state\nA) is silent  B) requires invalidating the other copies  C) leaves the line S  D) fetches the line from disk\n\n**16.** Under sequential consistency\nA) writes can be reordered freely  B) all processors see one global order that respects each program's order  C) caches are not allowed  D) only one core runs\n\n**17.** On x86, which reordering can be observed (total store order)?\nA) load-load  B) store-store  C) a store followed by a load to a different address  D) none\n\n**18.** On a GPU, memory accesses are fastest when\nA) the threads of a warp access adjacent addresses (coalesced)  B) every thread accesses a random address  C) all threads write the same address  D) data is in the disk cache\n\n**19.** An algorithm with arithmetic intensity below the ridge point of the roofline is\nA) compute bound  B) memory bound  C) impossible  D) latency bound only\n\n**20.** For a given task, lowering both voltage and frequency by the same factor generally\nA) reduces power and energy  B) increases power  C) leaves energy unchanged  D) increases energy\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** Machine A: 3 GHz, CPI 1.5, runs a program in 10^9 instructions. Machine B: 2 GHz, CPI 1.0, needs 1.2 × 10^9 instructions. Find the MIPS and the execution time of each and say which is faster.\n\n**B2. (5)** A program is 80 percent parallelisable. Find the Amdahl speedup on 4, 16 and infinitely many processors, and the parallel fraction needed for a speedup of 3 on 4 processors.\n\n**B3. (5)** For a 32 KB 8-way set-associative cache with 64-byte blocks and 32-bit addresses give the number of sets and the offset, index and tag widths. Is it possible to index it in parallel with a TLB for 4 KB pages? Explain.\n\n**B4. (5)** Two-level cache: L1 hit time 1 ns, miss rate 10 percent; L2 hit time 10 ns, local miss rate 20 percent; memory access 100 ns. Find the AMAT and the global L2 miss rate. If the base CPI is 1.0 with 30 percent loads/stores and 4 percent data miss rate with a 50-cycle penalty, find the CPI.\n\n**B5. (5)** Give the MESI states (C0, C1) and cumulative bus transactions after each access: C0 read, C1 read, C0 write, C1 read, C1 write, C0 read.\n\n**B6. (5)** A branch alternates T N T N T N T N. A 1-bit predictor starts predicting T; a 2-bit counter starts at 2 (weakly taken). Give the number of correct predictions of each over the 8 outcomes, and the behaviour of a history-based predictor.\n\n**B7. (5)** A GPU has 10 TFLOP/s peak and 500 GB/s bandwidth. Find the ridge point, the attainable performance of SAXPY (2 FLOP per 12 bytes) and the minimum time to add two vectors of 10^9 floats.\n\n**B8. (5)** A processor's voltage and frequency are both reduced by 15 percent. Find the relative dynamic power, the performance change and the relative energy for a fixed task.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. Pipelining.** (a) For the code `lw x1,0(x2); add x3,x1,x4; lw x5,0(x6); sub x7,x5,x3; sw x7,0(x8)` find the cycle counts in a five-stage pipeline without forwarding and with forwarding [4]. (b) Reorder it to remove the stall and give the new count [3]. (c) A pipeline has 25 percent loads (40 percent followed by a dependent instruction, 1 stall) and 20 percent branches (15 percent mispredicted, 3-cycle penalty): CPI and speedup over the unpipelined machine [3].\n\n**C2. Out-of-order execution.** Latencies ADD 1, MUL 3, DIV 5; one start per cycle.\n`I1 DIV R1,R2,R3; I2 ADD R4,R1,R5; I3 SUB R5,R6,R7; I4 MUL R1,R8,R9; I5 ADD R10,R4,R1`.\n(a) List the RAW, WAR and WAW dependences [3]. (b) Rename the registers and give the true dependence graph [3]. (c) With renaming, find the start and finish cycles for in-order issue and out-of-order issue and the total cycles [4].\n\n**C3. Caches.** A 64-byte cache with 16-byte blocks sees the byte addresses 0, 64, 0, 64, 16, 80, 16, 80, 0, 16.\n(a) Hit/miss pattern for a direct-mapped, a 2-way LRU and a fully associative cache [4]. (b) Classify the direct-mapped misses (compulsory, capacity, conflict) [2]. (c) A 4096 × 4096 int matrix summed row by row takes 0.012 s and column by column 0.49 s on a test machine: explain the difference and give a software remedy [4].\n\n**C4. Coherence and multicore.** (a) Trace MESI for two cores: C0 read, C0 write, C0 write, C1 write, C0 read, giving the states and the number of bus transactions [4]. (b) Two threads each increment their own counter; with the counters in the same cache line the run took 0.91 s, and with padding 0.48 s. Explain and fix [3]. (c) Define sequential consistency and give one reordering allowed by x86-TSO [3].\n\n**C5. GPUs, roofline and power.** (a) With 256 threads per block, how many blocks cover 10^6 elements and how many warps per block? [2] (b) Explain divergence and coalescing [3] (c) Attainable performance of a dot product (2 FLOP, 8 bytes) and why a large matrix multiplication reaches the peak on the roofline of B7 [3] (d) Why does heterogeneous (big.LITTLE and accelerator) design address the power wall? [2]\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **B** · 2 **B** · 3 **B** · 4 **B** · 5 **B** · 6 **B** · 7 **C** · 8 **B** · 9 **B** · 10 **B** · 11 **B** · 12 **B** · 13 **B** · 14 **A** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **C** · 18 **A** · 19 **B** · 20 **A**\n\nBrief reasons:\n1. A better compiler mainly changes the instruction count (IC) of the program.\n2. MIPS = f/(CPI × 10⁶) ignores how many instructions are needed, so equal MIPS can still mean different execution times.\n3. CMOS dynamic power P = αCV²f.\n4. With forwarding a load-use hazard costs one stall cycle, because the data exists only at the end of MEM.\n5. A branch target buffer stores the target addresses of recently taken branches.\n6. A 2-bit saturating counter changes its prediction only after two consecutive wrong outcomes.\n7. In Tomasulo's algorithm results are broadcast on the common data bus (CDB).\n8. WAW (and WAR) name dependences are removed by register renaming.\n9. A VLIW processor relies on the compiler to schedule independent operations.\n10. The reorder buffer lets instructions complete out of order but commit in program order.\n11. Step 1: sets = 65,536/(32 × 1) = 2048. Step 2: index = log₂ 2048 = 11 bits (offset 5 bits, tag 32 - 16 = 16 bits).\n12. A fully associative cache has no conflict misses (any block can go in any line).\n13. Larger blocks reduce compulsory misses (spatial locality) but increase the miss penalty and may increase conflict misses.\n14. Write-through with a write buffer lets the processor continue without stalling on stores.\n15. A write to a line in the S state must first invalidate the other copies.\n16. Sequential consistency: all processors see one global order that respects each program's order.\n17. Under x86 total store order a store can be delayed past a later load to a different address.\n18. GPU memory accesses are fastest when the threads of a warp access adjacent addresses (coalescing).\n19. Below the ridge point the memory system limits performance: the kernel is memory bound.\n20. Step 1: P ∝ V²f; scaling both by the same factor gives P ∝ s³. Step 2: the task takes 1/s times as long, so energy ∝ s², which is lower.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** Step 1 (A): MIPS = f/(CPI × 10⁶) = 3000/1.5 = **2000**; time = (10⁹ × 1.5)/(3 × 10⁹) = **0.5 s**.\nStep 2 (B): MIPS = 2000/1.0 = **2000**; time = (1.2 × 10⁹ × 1.0)/(2 × 10⁹) = **0.6 s**.\nStep 3: same MIPS but A is faster by 0.6/0.5 = **1.2 times**.\n\n**B2.** Speedup = 1/((1 - f) + f/n) with f = 0.8.\nStep 1: n = 4: 1/(0.2 + 0.2) = **2.5**. n = 16: 1/(0.2 + 0.05) = **4.0**. n → ∞: 1/0.2 = **5**.\nStep 2: for a speedup of 3 on 4 processors: (1 - f) + f/4 = 1/3, so 1 - 0.75f = 0.3333 and f = 0.6667/0.75 = **0.889**.\n\n**B3.** 32 KB, 8-way, 64-byte blocks, 32-bit addresses.\nStep 1: sets = 32,768/(64 × 8) = **64**.\nStep 2: offset = log₂ 64 = **6** bits; index = log₂ 64 = **6** bits; tag = 32 - 12 = **20** bits.\nStep 3: yes, it can be indexed in parallel with the TLB: index + offset = 12 bits equals the 12-bit page offset of 4 KB pages, which is the same in the virtual and physical address; the cache is indexed while the TLB translates, and the physical tag is compared afterwards (VIPT).\n\n**B4.** Step 1: AMAT = 1 + 0.1 × (10 + 0.2 × 100) = 1 + 0.1 × 30 = **4.0 ns**.\nStep 2: global L2 miss rate = 0.1 × 0.2 = **2 percent**.\nStep 3: CPI = 1 + 0.3 × 0.04 × 50 = 1 + 0.6 = **1.6**.\n\n**B5.** Step 1: C0 read: (E, I), 1 transaction. Step 2: C1 read: (S, S), 2. Step 3: C0 write: (M, I), 3 (invalidate). Step 4: C1 read: (S, S), 4 (supply and write-back). Step 5: C1 write: (I, M), 5 (invalidate). Step 6: C0 read: (S, S), 6.\n\n**B6.** Outcomes T N T N T N T N.\n1-bit (starts predicting T): outcome 1 (T) correct; outcome 2 (N) predicted T, wrong; outcome 3 (T) predicted N, wrong; and so on: only the first is correct: **1 of 8**.\n2-bit (starts at 2): T predicted T correct (counter 3); N predicted T wrong (2); T correct (3); N wrong (2); ...: correct on every T: **4 of 8**.\nA history-based predictor uses the last outcome: after T it learns N and after N it learns T, so it predicts almost **8 of 8** after a short warm-up (99 percent over long runs).\n\n**B7.** Step 1: ridge point = peak/bandwidth = 10 × 10¹²/(500 × 10⁹) = **20 FLOP/byte**.\nStep 2: SAXPY intensity = 2/12 = 0.167 FLOP/byte; attainable = 500 GB/s × 0.167 = **83 GFLOP/s** (memory bound).\nStep 3: vector add traffic = 3 × 4 B × 10⁹ = 12 GB; time = 12 GB/500 GB/s = **24 ms**.\n\n**B8.** Step 1: relative dynamic power = 0.85² × 0.85 = 0.85³ = **0.614** (a saving of 38.6 percent).\nStep 2: performance falls to 0.85: the task is 15 percent slower.\nStep 3: energy = power × time = 0.614/0.85 = **0.72** of the original (28 percent less energy).\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.** Code: lw x1,0(x2); add x3,x1,x4; lw x5,0(x6); sub x7,x5,x3; sw x7,0(x8).\n(a) Dependences: `add` needs x1 (first `lw`); `sub` needs x5 (second `lw`) and x3 (`add`); `sw` needs x7 (`sub`).\nWithout forwarding (a consumer's ID waits for the producer's WB):\nStep 1: `lw` IF 1, ID 2, EX 3, MEM 4, WB 5.\nStep 2: `add` needs x1 written in cycle 5: ID 5 (2 stalls), EX 6, MEM 7, WB 8.\nStep 3: second `lw`: ID 6, EX 7, MEM 8, WB 9.\nStep 4: `sub` needs x5 (WB 9) and x3 (WB 8): ID 9, EX 10, MEM 11, WB 12.\nStep 5: `sw` needs x7 (WB 12): ID 12, EX 13, MEM 14, WB 15.\nTotal **15 cycles**.\nWith forwarding: base 5 + 4 = 9 cycles, plus one load-use bubble before `add` and one before `sub` (the second `lw` immediately precedes it): **11 cycles**.\n(b) Reorder as `lw x1; lw x5; add x3,x1,x4; sub x7,x5,x3; sw x7`: each load now has an instruction between it and its use, so no stall: **9 cycles**.\n(c) CPI = 1 + (0.25 × 0.4 × 1) + (0.2 × 0.15 × 3) = 1 + 0.10 + 0.09 = **1.19**; speedup over the unpipelined machine = 5/1.19 = **4.2**.\n\n**C2.** Code: I1 DIV R1,R2,R3; I2 ADD R4,R1,R5; I3 SUB R5,R6,R7; I4 MUL R1,R8,R9; I5 ADD R10,R4,R1.\n(a) **RAW**: I2 reads R1 (from I1); I5 reads R4 (I2) and R1 (I4). **WAR**: I3 writes R5, which I2 reads; I4 writes R1, which I2 reads. **WAW**: I4 and I1 both write R1.\n(b) Renaming: I1: R1 → P1; I2: R4 → P4 (reads P1 and the old R5); I3: R5 → P5; I4: R1 → P6; I5: R10 → P10 (reads P4 and P6). True dependences: **I1 → I2 → I5** and **I4 → I5**. I3 is independent.\n(c) In-order issue (an instruction cannot start before the previous one):\nStep 1: I1 starts 1, finishes 5 (DIV, 5 cycles).\nStep 2: I2 needs P1 (usable at 6): starts 6, finishes 6.\nStep 3: I3 starts 7, finishes 7.\nStep 4: I4 (MUL, 3 cycles) starts 8, finishes 10; its result is usable at 11.\nStep 5: I5 needs P4 (usable at 7) and P6 (usable at 11): starts **11**, finishes 11.\nTotal **11 cycles**.\nOut-of-order issue:\nStep 6: I1 1 to 5; I3 starts 2 (finish 2); I4 starts 3 (finish 5, result usable at 6); I2 starts 6 (finish 6).\nStep 7: I5 needs P4 (usable at 7) and P6 (usable at 6): starts **7**, finishes 7.\nTotal **7 cycles**.\n\n**C3.**\n(a) Block addresses (address/16): 0, 4, 0, 4, 1, 5, 1, 5, 0, 1.\nDirect-mapped (4 sets): blocks 0 and 4 share set 0; blocks 1 and 5 share set 1: **MMMMMMMMMM** (10 misses).\n2-way LRU (2 sets): **MMHHMMHHHH** (4 misses).\nFully associative (4 lines): **MMHHMMHHHH** (4 misses).\n(b) Direct-mapped misses: 4 compulsory (first access to blocks 0, 4, 1, 5), 0 capacity (the 4 blocks used fit in the cache), 10 - 4 = **6 conflict**.\n(c) Row-major access walks consecutive addresses: each 64-byte line (16 ints) is used 16 times and the prefetcher streams ahead. Column access has a stride of 4096 × 4 = 16 KB, so every access misses (4 of the 64 bytes used, a new page each time, lines evicted before reuse): 0.49/0.012 = 41 times slower. Remedy: **loop interchange** so the inner loop runs along a row, or **blocking** so that tiles fit in the cache.\n\n**C4.**\n(a) Step 1: C0 read: (E, I), 1 transaction.\nStep 2: C0 write: the E → M upgrade is silent: (M, I), no transaction.\nStep 3: C0 write again: (M, I), none.\nStep 4: C1 write: invalidates C0's copy: (I, M), transaction 2.\nStep 5: C0 read: (S, S), transaction 3.\n**3 bus transactions** in total; writes by C0 while holding the line exclusively are free.\n(b) **False sharing**: the two counters lie in one 64-byte line, so each write invalidates the other core's copy and the line ping-pongs: 0.91 s against 0.48 s, 1.9 times slower. Fix: pad or align each counter to its own cache line (64 or 128 bytes), or keep counters in thread-local variables.\n(c) **Sequential consistency**: the result is as if all operations of all processors were executed in some single order that respects each processor's program order. x86-TSO allows a **store to be reordered after a later load to a different address** (the store waits in the store buffer while the load executes), for example the litmus test in which both processors can read 0.\n\n**C5.**\n(a) Step 1: blocks = ⌈10⁶/256⌉ = ⌈3906.25⌉ = **3907**. Step 2: warps per block = 256/32 = **8**.\n(b) **Divergence**: threads of a warp share one instruction stream, so when they take different branches the two paths run one after the other with inactive lanes masked: the warp takes the time of both. **Coalescing**: when the threads of a warp access consecutive addresses the hardware merges them into a few wide memory transactions; scattered accesses need many transactions and waste bandwidth.\n(c) Step 1: dot product intensity = 2/8 = 0.25 FLOP/byte; attainable = 500 GB/s × 0.25 = **125 GFLOP/s** (memory bound).\nStep 2: a large blocked matrix multiplication has an arithmetic intensity far above the ridge point of 20 FLOP/byte (each loaded tile is reused many times), so it is compute bound and reaches the **10 TFLOP/s** peak.\n(d) Power is limited, so energy should be spent where it is most useful: little cores and fixed-function accelerators do a task at a fraction of the energy of a big general-purpose core, and big cores run only latency-critical work. Performance per watt, not peak frequency, now decides the design.","notes":"# Computer Architecture: Lecture Notes\n\nComputer Organization showed how a simple processor and memory system work. This course asks how modern machines get their speed: deep pipelines, branch prediction, out-of-order superscalar execution, cache hierarchies, multicore coherence, vector units and GPUs, all under power limits. Calculations and simulations here were run with scripts; the experiments were compiled with gcc and measured on a real laptop (Intel Core i5-7300U: 2 cores, 4 hardware threads, 2.6 GHz base; L1 data cache 32 KB 8-way with 64-byte lines, L2 256 KB, L3 3 MB), so those timings are real but machine-specific. Prerequisites: Computer Organization, Digital Logic Design, C programming and Operating Systems.\n\n# Chapter 1: Performance and Cost\n\n## 1.1 The iron law\nCPU time = instruction count (IC) × cycles per instruction (CPI) × clock period = IC × CPI / f.\nEach factor belongs to a different layer: IC to the program, compiler and instruction set; CPI to the microarchitecture; clock rate to the circuit design and technology.\n\nExample. A program of 4 × 10^9 instructions on a 2.5 GHz processor with CPI 1.6 takes 4 × 10^9 × 1.6/(2.5 × 10^9) = **2.56 s**.\n\n**MIPS is a poor metric.** Machine A: 3 GHz, CPI 1.5. Machine B: 2 GHz, CPI 1.0. Both run at 2000 MIPS (A: 3000/1.5; B: 2000/1.0). If a program takes 10^9 instructions on A but 1.2 × 10^9 on B (a weaker instruction set), A needs 0.5 s and B needs 0.6 s: A is **1.2 times** faster although their MIPS are equal. Compare execution time on real workloads (benchmark suites such as SPEC CPU), never MIPS or clock rate alone.\n\n## 1.2 Amdahl's law\nIf a fraction f of execution time can be sped up by a factor s, the overall speedup is 1/((1 - f) + f/s).\n\nFor a program that is 80 percent parallelisable on n processors: n = 2: **1.67**; n = 4: **2.5**; n = 8: **3.33**; n = 16: **4.0**; n = 1000: 4.98; the limit is 1/0.2 = **5**. The serial fraction dominates. To get a speedup of 3 on 4 processors, the parallel fraction must be f = (1 - 1/3)/(1 - 1/4) = **0.889**.\n\nAnother use: speeding up a component that is used 40 percent of the time by a factor of 2 gives only 1/(0.6 + 0.4/2) = **1.25** overall.\n\n**Gustafson's law** looks at scaling the problem with the machine: with serial fraction s of the parallel run time, speedup = n - s (n - 1); for s = 0.1 and n = 16 it is **14.5**. Amdahl is pessimistic when the problem grows with the machine.\n\nPrinciples of design: make the common case fast; exploit parallelism at every level (instruction, data, thread); exploit locality; trade off with the **dependability** and cost.\n\n## 1.3 Power and the end of frequency scaling\nDynamic power P = alpha C V^2 f (alpha is the activity factor, C the switched capacitance). Raising frequency usually needs higher voltage, so power grows roughly as f^3. In the mid-2000s chips reached about 100 W per cm^2 (the **power wall**) and clock rates stopped near 3 to 4 GHz; designers moved to multicore and specialised accelerators. Example: reducing both voltage and frequency by 15 percent cuts power to 0.85^3 = **0.614** (a 38.6 percent saving) while performance falls 15 percent: the energy to finish a fixed task is 0.614/0.85 = **0.72** of before, so slowing down saves energy. Static (leakage) power also matters and is paid even when idle; chips use clock gating, power gating and **DVFS** (dynamic voltage and frequency scaling). **Dark silicon**: not all transistors can be on at once within the power budget, which motivates accelerators.\n\n# Chapter 2: Pipelining and Hazards\n\n## 2.1 The classic five-stage pipeline\nIF, ID, EX, MEM, WB. With n instructions and k stages the ideal time is k + n - 1 cycles, so for n = 1000, k = 5: 1004 cycles against 5000 unpipelined (speedup **4.98**). Pipeline registers add overhead: a 10 ns datapath split into five 2 ns stages plus 0.2 ns latch overhead has a 2.2 ns cycle, a speedup of 10/2.2 = **4.5**, not 5.\n\n## 2.2 Hazards\n- **Structural**: two instructions need the same resource (a single memory port); fixed by separate instruction and data caches or more ports.\n- **Data**: an instruction needs a result that is not ready yet. Types: **RAW** (read after write, the true dependence), **WAR** (anti-dependence) and **WAW** (output dependence). In an in-order pipeline only RAW hazards occur.\n- **Control**: the next instruction is unknown until a branch resolves.\n\n**Forwarding (bypassing)** passes the ALU or memory result directly to the EX stage of the next instruction. A **load-use hazard** cannot be removed by forwarding (the loaded data is available only at the end of MEM) and costs one stall cycle.\n\nWorked example (registers write in the first half of a cycle, read in the second):\n```\nlw  x1, 0(x2)\nadd x3, x1, x4\nsub x5, x3, x6\nsw  x5, 0(x7)\n```\nWithout forwarding each dependent instruction waits for the producer's write-back: 2 stalls for each of the three dependences, so **5 + 3 + 6 = 14 cycles**. With forwarding the only stall is the load-use bubble: **5 + 3 + 1 = 9 cycles**. A chain of four dependent ALU instructions takes 14 cycles without forwarding and **8** with it (no stalls).\n\n**Scheduling** removes stalls: the sequence `lw x1; lw x3; add x5,x1,x3; sw x5; add x7,x1,x1` takes 10 cycles with forwarding (one load-use stall before the add); moving the independent `add x7` between the loads and the dependent add gives `lw x1; lw x3; add x7,x1,x1; add x5,x1,x3; sw x5` in **9 cycles** with no stall.\n\n**Pipeline CPI** = ideal CPI + stalls per instruction. Example: 25 percent loads of which 40 percent are followed by a dependent instruction (1 stall), 20 percent branches with a 15 percent misprediction rate and a 3-cycle penalty: CPI = 1 + 0.25 × 0.4 × 1 + 0.2 × 0.15 × 3 = **1.19**; the real speedup of the 5-stage pipeline is 5/1.19 = **4.2**.\n\n# Chapter 3: Instruction-Level Parallelism\n\n## 3.1 Branch prediction\nBranches are about 15 to 20 percent of instructions; with a deep pipeline (15 to 20 stages) a misprediction costs 15 or more cycles, so predictors must be very accurate.\n\n**Static**: always not taken, backward taken forward not taken. **Dynamic**:\n- **1-bit predictor** per branch: predict what happened last time.\n- **2-bit saturating counter** (states 0, 1 = predict not taken; 2, 3 = predict taken): changes its prediction only after two consecutive surprises.\n- **Correlating (two-level) predictors**: use the recent global history of branch outcomes to index the table; **gshare** XORs history with the branch address; modern CPUs use TAGE and perceptron predictors.\n- **Branch target buffer (BTB)** caches branch target addresses; a **return address stack** predicts returns.\n\nResults from a simulation (initial predictor state: predict taken; 2-bit counter initially weakly taken):\n```\nPattern (loop branch)        1-bit    2-bit    gshare (4-bit history)\nT T T N repeating (4 iter)    50.2%    75.0%    98.5%\n9 T then N repeating          80.1%    90.0%    89.2%\nT N T N alternating            0.2%    50.0%    99.3%\nrandom                        52.8%    52.5%    52.8%\n```\nFor a loop that runs 10 iterations the 1-bit predictor is wrong twice per loop exit (once at the exit and once when re-entering), while the 2-bit counter is wrong only at the exit: 90 percent. History-based predictors learn the alternating and short-loop patterns almost perfectly; **random data cannot be predicted** (about 50 percent).\n\nFirst 12 outcomes of T T T N: the 1-bit predictor is wrong at positions 4, 5, 8, 9, 12 (trace `cccWWccWWccW`); the 2-bit counter is wrong only at the N outcomes, 4, 8, 12 (trace `cccWcccWcccW`).\n\n**Cost of mispredictions**: CPI = base CPI + branch frequency × misprediction rate × penalty. For a 2-wide machine with base CPI 0.5, 20 percent branches and a 15-cycle penalty: 90 percent accuracy gives 0.5 + 0.2 × 0.1 × 15 = **0.8**; 95 percent gives **0.65**.\n\n**Measured effect** (C loop `if (d[i] >= 128) s += d[i]` over 16 million random values from 0 to 255, repeated 10 times, compiled to a real branch): unsorted data took **0.95 s**, the same data sorted took **0.24 s**: **4.0 times** faster, only because the branch becomes predictable. (With the compiler's conditional-move optimisation the difference disappears: the branch is removed.)\n\n## 3.2 Dynamic scheduling\nIn-order issue stalls on any dependence. **Out-of-order (OoO) execution** issues instructions as soon as their operands are ready, while still **committing in program order**.\n\nKey mechanisms:\n- **Register renaming** removes WAR and WAW (false) dependences by mapping architectural registers onto a larger pool of physical registers.\n- **Reservation stations / issue queue**: instructions wait for operands, results are broadcast on a common data bus (Tomasulo's algorithm).\n- **Reorder buffer (ROB)**: holds results until commit in order, giving **precise exceptions** and allowing **speculation** (execute past an unresolved branch; discard on a misprediction).\n- **Load/store queue**: orders memory operations and forwards store data to later loads.\n\nWorked example (single issue, one new instruction can start per cycle; latencies ADD 1, MUL 3, DIV 5; an instruction starts at the first cycle its sources are ready):\n```\nI1  DIV R1, R2, R3\nI2  ADD R4, R1, R5     depends on I1\nI3  ADD R6, R7, R8\nI4  MUL R9, R7, R8\nI5  ADD R10, R4, R6    depends on I2, I3\n```\n**In-order**: I1 starts at cycle 1 and finishes at 5; I2 waits until 6; I3 cannot start before I2 (cycle 7); I4 at 8 (finishes 10); I5 at 9: done at cycle **10**. **Out-of-order**: I3 starts at 2, I4 at 3 (finishes 5), I2 at 6, I5 at 7: done at cycle **7**. OoO hides the 5-cycle divide with independent work: 30 percent faster. Even with two issues per cycle the critical path (I1 to I2 to I5 = 5 + 1 + 1) still gives 7.\n\n**Superscalar**: issue several instructions per cycle. An n-wide machine has an ideal CPI of 1/n (a 4-wide machine, 0.25). Practical IPC is limited by dependences, branch prediction, cache misses and instruction fetch bandwidth, typically 1 to 3. Modern cores (6 to 8 wide, ROB of several hundred entries) still extract only a few instructions per cycle on typical code: the **ILP wall**.\n\n**VLIW and EPIC**: the compiler packs independent operations into long instruction words; simple hardware, but sensitive to unpredictable latencies and binary compatibility.\n\n**Simultaneous multithreading (SMT / Hyper-Threading)** runs instructions from two or more threads in the same core to fill unused issue slots. On the test laptop (2 cores, 4 hardware threads), a fully parallel floating-point loop sped up only 1.4 to 1.5 times with 2 threads and 1.7 times with 4: SMT threads share the core's execution units.\n\n# Chapter 4: The Memory Hierarchy\n\n## 4.1 Locality and hierarchy\n**Temporal locality** (recently used items are used again) and **spatial locality** (nearby items are used soon) make a small fast memory in front of a large slow one effective. Typical latencies: registers under 1 ns; L1 cache about 1 to 2 ns (4 cycles); L2 about 4 to 6 ns; L3 about 15 to 60 ns; DRAM about 80 to 120 ns; SSD about 100 μs; disk about 10 ms.\n\n**Measured on the test laptop** (a pointer-chasing loop in which every load depends on the previous one, so the time per load is the true latency, for working sets of increasing size):\n```\nworking set     latency per load\n4 KB            1.9 ns     (L1 hit, 4 to 5 cycles)\n32 KB           1.5 ns\n64 KB           3.5 ns     (spills out of the 32 KB L1)\n256 KB          6.0 ns     (L2)\n1 MB           14.0 ns     (L3)\n4 MB           57.7 ns     (larger than the 3 MB L3: mostly DRAM)\n16 MB         107.5 ns\n64 MB         125.3 ns     (DRAM)\n```\nThe steps in the curve reveal the cache sizes: a 60-fold difference between L1 and DRAM.\n\n## 4.2 Cache organisation\nA cache holds blocks (lines) of 2^b bytes. An address splits into **tag | index | block offset**.\n- **Direct mapped**: each block maps to exactly one set: index = (block address) mod (number of sets).\n- **n-way set associative**: a block may be in any of n lines of its set (compare n tags in parallel).\n- **Fully associative**: any line (one set).\nNumber of sets = cache size/(block size × associativity). Address breakdown examples (32-bit addresses):\n- 4 KB direct-mapped, 16-byte blocks: 256 sets, offset **4** bits, index **8** bits, tag **20** bits.\n- 32 KB 8-way, 64-byte blocks: 64 sets, offset **6**, index **6**, tag **20**.\n- 64 KB 2-way, 64-byte blocks: 512 sets, offset **6**, index **9**, tag **17**.\nEach line also has a valid bit (and a dirty bit for write-back).\n\n**Replacement**: LRU (or an approximation such as pseudo-LRU), random, FIFO. **Write policies**: write-through (every write goes to the next level; simple, needs a write buffer) versus write-back (write only the cache, set the dirty bit and write the block when evicted; less traffic, the standard choice); **write-allocate** (bring the block on a write miss) versus no-write-allocate.\n\n## 4.3 Worked trace: placement matters\nA 64-byte cache with 16-byte blocks (4 blocks) and the byte-address sequence 0, 64, 0, 64, 16, 80, 16, 80, 0, 16:\n- **Direct-mapped** (4 sets): 0 and 64 map to set 0; 16 and 80 map to set 1; each pair evicts the other every time: **10 misses of 10** (all misses).\n- **2-way set associative** (2 sets): 0, 64 are both kept in set 0, and 16, 80 in set 1: pattern MMHHMMHHHH, **4 misses** (6 hits).\n- **Fully associative** (4 lines): the same, **4 misses**.\n**The three Cs**: **compulsory** misses (first access; 4 here), **capacity** misses (the working set does not fit), **conflict** misses (too little associativity: the 6 extra misses of the direct-mapped cache). Doubling associativity removes conflict misses but increases hit time and energy.\n\nA simple loop reading 1024 consecutive 4-byte integers with 64-byte blocks has 1 miss per 16 accesses (miss rate **6.25 percent**: 64 compulsory misses).\n\n## 4.4 Average memory access time\nAMAT = hit time + miss rate × miss penalty. Example: hit time 1 ns, miss rate 5 percent, penalty 100 ns: **6 ns**. With two levels: AMAT = t(L1) + m(L1) × (t(L2) + m(L2 local) × t(memory)). With L1 1 ns and 10 percent miss rate, L2 10 ns with 20 percent local miss rate, memory 100 ns: AMAT = 1 + 0.1 × (10 + 0.2 × 100) = **4.0 ns** (the global L2 miss rate is 0.1 × 0.2 = 2 percent).\nCPI with memory stalls: base CPI 1.0, 30 percent loads/stores with a 4 percent miss rate and a 50-cycle penalty: CPI = 1 + 0.3 × 0.04 × 50 = **1.6**; if every instruction also fetches with a 2 percent instruction-miss rate: 1 + (0.02 + 0.3 × 0.04) × 50 = **2.6**.\n\n## 4.5 Software and hardware optimisations\n- **Loop interchange and blocking (tiling)**: traverse data in the order it is stored and work on tiles that fit in the cache.\n- **Measured**: summing a 4096 × 4096 matrix of ints (64 MB) row by row (contiguous) took **0.012 to 0.025 s**; column by column (stride 16 KB) took **0.49 to 0.50 s**: **20 to 40 times slower** for exactly the same arithmetic. Every column access touches a new cache line and a new page.\n- Hardware: **prefetching** (next-line, stride), non-blocking caches (several outstanding misses, \"miss under miss\"), way prediction, victim caches, critical-word-first.\n- **Virtual memory interplay**: a TLB lookup runs in parallel with the cache index (virtually indexed, physically tagged L1 requires cache size/associativity <= page size: 32 KB / 8 ways = 4 KB = one page).\n\n# Chapter 5: Multiprocessors and Cache Coherence\n\n## 5.1 Why multicore\nSingle-thread performance stalled (power and ILP walls); chip makers put several cores on a die. The software must supply **thread-level parallelism**. Amdahl's law applies: the serial part limits the speedup.\n\n## 5.2 Cache coherence\nEach core has a private cache, so several copies of one block can exist. The system must make writes visible: **a read returns the most recently written value** and writes to one location are seen in the same order by everyone. **Snooping protocols** (small systems, a shared bus or ring) broadcast; **directory protocols** (large systems) keep a record of which caches hold each block.\n\n**MESI** (the most common): each cache line is **M**odified (dirty, only copy), **E**xclusive (clean, only copy), **S**hared (clean, possibly several copies) or **I**nvalid. Rules: a read miss with no other copy gives E, otherwise S (an M copy elsewhere is written back and both become S); a write to S or I **invalidates** the other copies (bus transaction) and gives M; a write to E becomes M silently (no bus traffic).\n\nTrace for two cores (C0, C1) on one block, giving the states (C0 C1) and the cumulative number of bus transactions:\n```\nC0 read    E I    1\nC1 read    S S    2\nC0 write   M I    3   (invalidate C1)\nC1 read    S S    4   (C0 writes back, both shared)\nC1 write   I M    5   (invalidate C0)\nC0 read    S S    6\n```\nand for a core that keeps the block to itself: C0 read (E, 1 transaction), C0 write (M, **no** transaction), C0 write (M, none), C1 write (I M, 2), C0 read (S S, 3). Writes to a block already exclusive are free; **sharing is expensive**.\n\n**False sharing**: two threads write **different** variables that happen to lie in the same cache line; the line ping-pongs between the cores although no data is really shared. **Measured**: two threads each incrementing their own 8-byte counter 200 million times took **0.91 to 0.94 s** when the counters were adjacent (same 64-byte line) but **0.48 to 0.50 s** when padded 128 bytes apart: **1.9 times slower**. Fix: pad or align per-thread data to the cache-line size.\n\n## 5.3 Memory consistency and synchronisation\nA **memory consistency model** defines the order in which one core's writes appear to others. **Sequential consistency** (as if operations were interleaved in program order) is the easiest to reason about but forbids many optimisations; real machines are weaker (x86 uses **TSO**: stores can be delayed by a store buffer; ARM and RISC-V are weaker still), so programmers use atomic operations (compare-and-swap, fetch-and-add, load-linked/store-conditional), **fences**, and locks. Peterson's algorithm needs sequentially consistent atomics to work.\n\n**Taxonomy (Flynn)**: SISD, SIMD (vector, GPU), MISD, MIMD (multicore). **Shared-memory** multiprocessors: **UMA** (uniform) versus **NUMA** (a core accesses its local memory faster than remote); message-passing clusters; warehouse-scale computers.\n\n# Chapter 6: Data-Level Parallelism and GPUs\n\n## 6.1 SIMD and vector processing\nOne instruction operates on a vector of elements. x86 SSE/AVX (128/256/512-bit), ARM NEON/SVE, RISC-V vector extension. A 256-bit AVX register holds 8 single-precision floats, so one instruction does 8 additions. Compilers **auto-vectorise** simple loops; data must be contiguous and aligned and the loop free of dependences (and aliasing).\n\n## 6.2 GPU architecture\nA GPU has many simple cores grouped in **streaming multiprocessors**; threads are organised in **blocks**; the hardware executes groups of 32 threads (**warps**) in lock-step (**SIMT**): one instruction stream, many threads. Key ideas:\n- **Massive multithreading hides latency**: when a warp waits for memory, another warp runs (no big caches or branch predictors needed).\n- **Branch divergence**: if threads of a warp take different paths the paths run one after the other, wasting lanes.\n- **Coalescing**: adjacent threads should access adjacent addresses so a warp's loads combine into few memory transactions.\n- Memory hierarchy: registers, shared memory (software-managed, per block), L2, high-bandwidth global memory.\nFor a vector of 10^6 elements and 256 threads per block you launch ceil(10^6/256) = **3907** blocks.\n\n## 6.3 The roofline model\nAttainable performance = min(peak compute, memory bandwidth × arithmetic intensity), where **arithmetic intensity** = floating-point operations per byte moved from memory. For a machine with 10 TFLOP/s peak and 500 GB/s: the **ridge point** is 10 × 10^12/(500 × 10^9) = **20 FLOP/byte**.\n- SAXPY (y = a x + y, 2 FLOP and 12 bytes per element): intensity 0.167 gives min(10 TFLOP/s, 500 GB/s × 0.167) = **83 GFLOP/s**: **memory bound**.\n- A dot product (2 FLOP, 8 bytes): 0.25 gives **125 GFLOP/s**.\n- A large blocked matrix multiplication with intensity above 20 is **compute bound** and reaches the peak.\nVector addition on 10^9 floats moves 12 GB: at 500 GB/s the GPU needs at least **24 ms**; a CPU with dual-channel DDR4-3200 (3200 × 10^6 × 8 B × 2 = 51.2 GB/s) needs at least **234 ms**. Moving data over PCIe may cost more than the computation: GPUs pay off only with high arithmetic intensity and enough parallelism.\n\n# Chapter 7: Specialisation and Energy\nWith Dennard scaling over, performance per watt improves through **domain-specific architectures**: tensor/matrix engines for neural networks (a systolic array performs thousands of multiply-accumulates per cycle with local data movement), video codecs, cryptographic engines, FPGAs. Energy facts: an off-chip DRAM access costs roughly 100 to 1000 times the energy of an arithmetic operation, so reducing data movement matters more than reducing arithmetic. **Heterogeneous** chips combine big (fast) and little (efficient) cores (big.LITTLE) with GPUs and accelerators.\n\n## Quick reference\n- CPU time = IC × CPI / f. Amdahl: 1/((1 - f) + f/s). Pipeline CPI = 1 + stalls.\n- AMAT = hit + miss rate × penalty; sets = size/(block × ways); tag bits = 32 - index - offset.\n- Branch penalty CPI = f_branch × miss rate × penalty. N-wide ideal CPI = 1/N.\n- Roofline: attainable = min(peak, bandwidth × intensity). Dynamic power = alpha C V^2 f.\n","plan":"# Computer Architecture: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 11 hours per week (3 reading, 6 to 7 problems, simulation and measurement, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Architecture is quantitative: for every idea ask \"how many cycles, how many misses, how many watts?\" and check the answer by simulation or by timing a real program.\n\n## Textbooks\n- John Hennessy and David Patterson, *Computer Architecture: A Quantitative Approach*. The graduate-level standard; the structure of this course follows its chapters.\n- David Patterson and John Hennessy, *Computer Organization and Design* (RISC-V edition) for review of the basics.\n- Randal Bryant and David O'Hallaron, *Computer Systems: A Programmer's Perspective* (caches and performance from the programmer's side).\n- Daniel Sorin, Mark Hill and David Wood, *A Primer on Memory Consistency and Cache Coherence* (free).\n- Ulrich Drepper, *What Every Programmer Should Know About Memory* (free paper).\n\n## Free courses and tools\n- MIT 6.823 Computer System Architecture; ETH Zurich Digital Design and Computer Architecture (Onur Mutlu's lectures, free on YouTube); Berkeley CS152.\n- Simulators: **gem5** (cycle-level CPU and memory simulator), Ripes (visual RISC-V pipeline simulator), Sniper, ChampSim (cache and branch-predictor studies), CACTI (cache area/time/energy model).\n- Measurement tools on Linux: `perf stat` (cycles, instructions, cache misses, branch misses), `lscpu`, `valgrind --tool=cachegrind`, Intel VTune, `likwid`.\n- For GPUs: CUDA or OpenCL tutorials (Google Colab gives a free GPU), NVIDIA Nsight.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Performance, the iron law, Amdahl, power (Ch 1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1 to Q4, Q7. Time a program and compute CPI with `perf stat`.\n\n**Week 2: Pipelining and hazards (Ch 2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q5, Q6. Simulate the hazard examples in Ripes with and without forwarding.\n\n**Week 3: Branch prediction (Ch 3.1).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1, Q2, Q3. Write a Python simulator for 1-bit, 2-bit and gshare predictors and reproduce the table in the notes. Reproduce the sorted versus unsorted array loop in C.\n\n**Week 4: Dynamic scheduling, renaming, the reorder buffer (Ch 3.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q4 to Q7. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week (Chapters 1 to 3 plus the start of caches).\n\n**Week 5: Cache organisation and mapping (Ch 4.1, 4.2).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1, Q2. Write a cache simulator (size, block, associativity, LRU) and run the trace examples.\n\n**Week 6: Cache performance and optimisation (Ch 4.3 to 4.5).** Do: Assignment 3 Q3, Q4. Measure the row-versus-column traversal and the pointer-chasing latency curve on your own machine.\n\n**Week 7: Virtual memory, TLBs, advanced memory topics.** Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam.\n\n**Week 8: Multicore and cache coherence (Ch 5.1, 5.2).** Do: Assignment 3 Q5. Write a MESI simulator and reproduce the bus-transaction counts; measure false sharing.\n\n**Week 9: Consistency, synchronisation, SMT and NUMA (Ch 5.3).** Do: litmus tests (store buffering) in C with threads; read the x86-TSO paper summary.\n\n**Week 10: SIMD and vector processing (Ch 6.1).** Do: write a loop and check auto-vectorisation with `gcc -O3 -fopt-info-vec`; time scalar versus AVX intrinsics.\n\n**Week 11: GPUs and the roofline model (Ch 6.2, 6.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q6, Q7. Run a vector-add kernel on a free Colab GPU and compare with the CPU; draw a roofline for your machine.\n\n**Week 12: Energy, accelerators, domain-specific architectures (Ch 7).** Read about the TPU and systolic arrays; estimate the energy of data movement versus arithmetic.\n\n**Week 13: Project.** Use gem5 or your own simulator to study one design question (for example the effect of cache size, associativity or predictor type on a benchmark) and write a short report with graphs and a conclusion.\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo missed Class Test and assignment questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, formula sheet only).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can apply the iron law and Amdahl's and Gustafson's laws and explain why MIPS is misleading.\n- [ ] I can find hazards and count cycles in a pipeline with and without forwarding, and reorder code to avoid stalls.\n- [ ] I can simulate 1-bit, 2-bit and history-based branch predictors and compute the CPI effect.\n- [ ] I can explain register renaming, reservation stations and the reorder buffer and schedule instructions in-order and out-of-order.\n- [ ] I can split an address into tag, index and offset for any cache and simulate hits and misses.\n- [ ] I can compute AMAT and CPI with memory stalls and classify misses (compulsory, capacity, conflict).\n- [ ] I can trace MESI states and explain false sharing and memory consistency.\n- [ ] I can explain GPU execution (warps, divergence, coalescing) and use the roofline model.\n- [ ] I can estimate the effect of voltage and frequency changes on power and energy.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with a formula sheet only.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. Build small simulators; they make every concept concrete and let you check the exam-style answers.\n2. Measure real hardware: `perf stat` and timing loops often contradict first intuitions.\n3. Always state assumptions (latencies, issue width, hit times) before computing.\n4. Draw timing diagrams and cache state tables by hand for every new mechanism.\n5. Read about real designs (Intel, AMD, ARM, Apple, NVIDIA) after learning each idea and compare their numbers to your estimates.\n","test":"# Computer Architecture: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each).\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** CPU execution time equals\nA) IC × f / CPI  B) IC × CPI / f  C) CPI × f / IC  D) IC / (CPI × f)\n\n**2.** Which hazard still needs a one-cycle stall in a five-stage pipeline even with full forwarding?\nA) ALU result used by the next ALU instruction  B) a store after an ALU instruction  C) a load result used by the next instruction  D) a branch after an ALU instruction\n\n**3.** A WAR (write-after-read) dependence means that\nA) a later instruction writes a register that an earlier instruction reads  B) a later instruction reads a value an earlier one writes  C) two instructions write the same register  D) two instructions read the same register\n\n**4.** Register renaming removes\nA) RAW dependences  B) structural hazards  C) cache misses  D) WAR and WAW dependences\n\n**5.** The reorder buffer allows an out-of-order processor to\nA) issue instructions faster  B) commit results in program order and give precise exceptions  C) predict branches  D) avoid cache misses\n\n**6.** By Amdahl's law the maximum speedup from any number of processors is limited by\nA) the serial fraction of the program  B) the clock rate  C) the cache size  D) the number of instructions\n\n**7.** On a loop that runs 10 iterations, a 2-bit saturating counter mispredicts the loop branch about\nA) never  B) twice per loop  C) once per loop  D) ten times per loop\n\n**8.** A 1-bit predictor on the alternating pattern T N T N ... is\nA) always right  B) right 50 percent of the time  C) right 75 percent of the time  D) almost always wrong\n\n**9.** Which dependence is a true data dependence?\nA) WAR  B) RAW  C) WAW  D) none of these\n\n**10.** The ideal CPI of a 4-wide superscalar processor is\nA) 4  B) 1  C) 0.25  D) 0.5\n\n**11.** A compulsory miss is\nA) the first access to a block  B) a miss caused by too little associativity  C) a miss caused by the cache being too small  D) a miss in the TLB\n\n**12.** Conflict misses are reduced by\nA) a bigger clock  B) larger blocks only  C) a deeper pipeline  D) higher associativity\n\n**13.** Average memory access time is\nA) miss rate × miss penalty  B) hit time + miss rate × miss penalty  C) hit time × miss rate  D) hit time + miss penalty\n\n**14.** A write-back cache writes a block to the next level\nA) on every store  B) only when the program ends  C) when a dirty block is replaced  D) never\n\n**15.** A 32 KB 4-way set-associative cache with 64-byte blocks has how many sets?\nA) 128  B) 256  C) 512  D) 64\n\n**16.** In MESI, the E state means the line is\nA) dirty and shared  B) clean and the only cached copy  C) invalid  D) dirty and exclusive\n\n**17.** False sharing occurs when\nA) two cores read the same variable  B) a lock is held too long  C) two threads use the same variable without synchronisation  D) different variables written by different cores lie in the same cache line\n\n**18.** A GPU warp is\nA) a group of 32 threads executing in lock-step  B) a cache line  C) a memory bank  D) a block of 1024 threads\n\n**19.** Branch divergence in a warp causes\nA) faster execution  B) cache misses  C) the paths to execute one after the other (serialisation)  D) a deadlock\n\n**20.** In the roofline model attainable performance is\nA) peak compute only  B) the minimum of peak compute and bandwidth × arithmetic intensity  C) bandwidth only  D) the maximum of the two\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Performance (10 marks)** Machine X: 4 GHz, CPI 2.0, executes 1.2 × 10^9 instructions for a program. Machine Y: 3 GHz, CPI 1.0, executes 1.5 × 10^9 instructions for the same program. (a) Execution time and MIPS of each [4]. (b) Which is faster and by what factor? [2] (c) 70 percent of a program can be parallelised: the Amdahl speedup on 4 and 8 processors [4].\n\n**B2. Pipelining (10 marks)** (a) For `add x1,x2,x3; sub x4,x1,x5; and x6,x1,x4; or x7,x1,x6` in a five-stage pipeline (registers written in the first half and read in the second half), find the number of cycles without forwarding and with forwarding [5]. (b) Loads are 20 percent of instructions and half of them are followed by a dependent instruction (1 stall); branches are 15 percent with 10 percent mispredicted and a 4-cycle penalty. Find the CPI [3]. (c) Name the three hazard types [2].\n\n**B3. Branch prediction (10 marks)** A branch outcome sequence is T T T N repeated. A 1-bit predictor starts predicting T; a 2-bit saturating counter starts at 2 (weakly taken). (a) Give the prediction results (c or W) of both for the first 12 outcomes [6]. (b) Find the steady-state accuracy of each [2]. (c) Which type of predictor would predict this pattern almost perfectly and why? [2]\n\n**B4. Caches (10 marks)** A 64-byte cache has 16-byte blocks. The byte addresses 0, 64, 0, 64, 16, 80, 16, 80, 0, 16 are accessed. (a) Hit/miss sequence for a direct-mapped cache and for a 2-way LRU cache [4]. (b) The hit time is 1 cycle and the miss penalty 20 cycles: the AMAT of each [3]. (c) For 32-bit addresses give the offset, index and tag bits for an 8 KB 2-way cache with 32-byte blocks [3].\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **B** · 2 **C** · 3 **A** · 4 **D** · 5 **B** · 6 **A** · 7 **C** · 8 **D** · 9 **B** · 10 **C** · 11 **A** · 12 **D** · 13 **B** · 14 **C** · 15 **A** · 16 **B** · 17 **D** · 18 **A** · 19 **C** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons:\n1. The iron law: time = instruction count × CPI × cycle time = IC × CPI / f.\n2. Load data exists only at the end of the MEM stage, so even with forwarding an instruction using it in the next cycle must wait one cycle (load-use hazard).\n3. WAR: a later instruction writes a register that an earlier instruction reads.\n4. Renaming gives each write a new physical register, removing the false (name) dependences WAR and WAW; true RAW dependences remain.\n5. The reorder buffer holds results until they can commit in program order, which gives precise exceptions.\n6. Amdahl's law: the speedup is limited by 1/(serial fraction).\n7. The 2-bit counter stays on the taken side through the loop and is wrong only at the loop exit: once per loop.\n8. Step 1: a 1-bit predictor predicts the previous outcome. Step 2: on T N T N each outcome differs from the previous, so it is almost always wrong.\n9. RAW (read-after-write) is the true data dependence; WAR and WAW are name dependences.\n10. Step 1: a 4-wide machine completes 4 instructions per cycle. Step 2: ideal CPI = 1/4 = 0.25.\n11. A compulsory miss is the first access to a block.\n12. Conflict misses are reduced by higher associativity.\n13. AMAT = hit time + miss rate × miss penalty.\n14. A write-back cache writes a block to the next level when a dirty block is replaced.\n15. Step 1: sets = capacity/(block size × ways). Step 2: 32,768/(64 × 4) = 32,768/256 = 128.\n16. E (exclusive) means the line is clean and the only cached copy.\n17. False sharing: different variables written by different cores lie in the same cache line, so the line ping-pongs although no variable is shared.\n18. A warp is a group of 32 threads executing in lock-step.\n19. Divergence in a warp makes the two paths execute one after the other (serialisation).\n20. Roofline: attainable performance = min(peak compute, bandwidth × arithmetic intensity).\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) Step 1 (X): time = IC × CPI / f = (1.2 × 10⁹ × 2.0)/(4 × 10⁹) = 2.4/4 = **0.6 s**.\nStep 2: MIPS = f/(CPI × 10⁶) = 4000/2.0 = **2000**.\nStep 3 (Y): time = (1.5 × 10⁹ × 1.0)/(3 × 10⁹) = **0.5 s**; MIPS = 3000/1.0 = **3000**.\n(b) Y is faster: 0.6/0.5 = **1.2 times** faster.\n(c) Amdahl with f = 0.7: speedup = 1/(0.3 + 0.7/n).\nStep 1: n = 4: 1/(0.3 + 0.175) = 1/0.475 = **2.105**.\nStep 2: n = 8: 1/(0.3 + 0.0875) = 1/0.3875 = **2.581**.\n\n**B2.**\n(a) Code: add x1,x2,x3; sub x4,x1,x5; and x6,x1,x4; or x7,x1,x6. Dependences: sub reads x1 (from add); and reads x1 (add) and x4 (sub); or reads x1 (add) and x6 (and).\nWithout forwarding (each consumer's ID waits for the producer's WB):\nStep 1: add: IF 1, ID 2, EX 3, MEM 4, WB 5.\nStep 2: sub needs x1 written in cycle 5, so its ID is in cycle 5 (2 stalls): ID 5, EX 6, MEM 7, WB 8.\nStep 3: and needs x4 written in cycle 8, so ID 8 (2 more stalls): EX 9, MEM 10, WB 11.\nStep 4: or needs x6 written in cycle 11, so ID 11: EX 12, MEM 13, WB 14.\nTotal **14 cycles**. (Formula: 5 + 3 instructions + 6 stalls.)\nWith forwarding no stalls are needed (ALU results are forwarded): 5 + 3 = **8 cycles**.\n(b) CPI = 1 + (load fraction × dependent fraction × 1 stall) + (branch fraction × mispredict rate × penalty) = 1 + 0.2 × 0.5 × 1 + 0.15 × 0.1 × 4 = 1 + 0.10 + 0.06 = **1.16**.\n(c) The three hazard types: **structural** (resource conflicts), **data** (RAW, WAR, WAW) and **control** (branches).\n\n**B3.**\n(a) Outcomes: T T T N T T T N T T T N.\nStep 1 (1-bit, predicts the previous outcome, initially T): outcomes 1 to 3 correct; outcome 4 (N) predicted T, wrong; outcome 5 (T) predicted N, wrong; outcomes 6 and 7 correct; outcome 8 (N) wrong; outcome 9 (T) wrong; outcomes 10 and 11 correct; outcome 12 (N) wrong.\n1-bit: **c c c W W c c W W c c W**.\nStep 2 (2-bit counter starting at 2, taken when ≥ 2): the counter stays at 3 for T T T, falls to 2 on N (still predicting taken), and returns to 3 on the next T.\n2-bit: **c c c W c c c W c c c W**.\n(b) Steady state: the 1-bit predictor is wrong twice per 4 outcomes: **50 percent**. The 2-bit predictor is wrong once per 4: **75 percent**.\n(c) A **history-based (two-level or gshare) predictor**: with the last 3 or 4 outcomes as part of the index, the history T T T always precedes N and the other histories precede T, so each context has a stable prediction: about 98 percent after warm-up.\n\n**B4.**\n(a) Block addresses (address/16): 0, 4, 0, 4, 1, 5, 1, 5, 0, 1.\nDirect-mapped (4 sets): blocks 0 and 4 share set 0, blocks 1 and 5 share set 1, so every access misses: **M M M M M M M M M M** (10 misses).\n2-way (2 sets): blocks 0 and 4 fit in set 0 and blocks 1 and 5 in set 1: M M H H M M H H H H (**4 misses**).\n(b) Step 1: direct-mapped miss rate = 10/10 = 1.0; AMAT = 1 + 1.0 × 20 = **21 cycles**.\nStep 2: 2-way miss rate = 4/10 = 0.4; AMAT = 1 + 0.4 × 20 = 1 + 8 = **9 cycles**.\n(c) Step 1: sets = 8192/(32 × 2) = 8192/64 = 128.\nStep 2: offset = log₂ 32 = **5** bits; index = log₂ 128 = **7** bits; tag = 32 - 7 - 5 = **20** bits."},mcq:[{"q":"CPU execution time equals","options":["IC × f / CPI","IC × CPI / f","CPI × f / IC","IC / (CPI × f)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The iron law: time = instruction count × CPI × cycle time = IC × CPI / f.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which hazard still needs a one-cycle stall in a five-stage pipeline even with full forwarding?","options":["ALU result used by the next ALU instruction","a store after an ALU instruction","a load result used by the next instruction","a branch after an ALU instruction"],"answer":2,"explain":"Load data exists only at the end of the MEM stage, so even with forwarding an instruction using it in the next cycle must wait one cycle (load-use hazard).","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A WAR (write-after-read) dependence means that","options":["a later instruction writes a register that an earlier instruction reads","a later instruction reads a value an earlier one writes","two instructions write the same register","two instructions read the same register"],"answer":0,"explain":"WAR: a later instruction writes a register that an earlier instruction reads.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Register renaming removes","options":["RAW dependences","structural hazards","cache misses","WAR and WAW dependences"],"answer":3,"explain":"Renaming gives each write a new physical register, removing the false (name) dependences WAR and WAW; true RAW dependences remain.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The reorder buffer allows an out-of-order processor to","options":["issue instructions faster","commit results in program order and give precise exceptions","predict branches","avoid cache misses"],"answer":1,"explain":"The reorder buffer holds results until they can commit in program order, which gives precise exceptions.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"By Amdahl's law the maximum speedup from any number of processors is limited by","options":["the serial fraction of the program","the clock rate","the cache size","the number of instructions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Amdahl's law: the speedup is limited by 1/(serial fraction).","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"On a loop that runs 10 iterations, a 2-bit saturating counter mispredicts the loop branch about","options":["never","twice per loop","once per loop","ten times per loop"],"answer":2,"explain":"The 2-bit counter stays on the taken side through the loop and is wrong only at the loop exit: once per loop.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A 1-bit predictor on the alternating pattern T N T N ... is","options":["always right","right 50 percent of the time","right 75 percent of the time","almost always wrong"],"answer":3,"explain":"Step 1: a 1-bit predictor predicts the previous outcome. Step 2: on T N T N each outcome differs from the previous, so it is almost always wrong.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which dependence is a true data dependence?","options":["WAR","RAW","WAW","none of these"],"answer":1,"explain":"RAW (read-after-write) is the true data dependence; WAR and WAW are name dependences.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The ideal CPI of a 4-wide superscalar processor is","options":["4","1","0.25","0.5"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: a 4-wide machine completes 4 instructions per cycle. Step 2: ideal CPI = 1/4 = 0.25.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A compulsory miss is","options":["the first access to a block","a miss caused by too little associativity","a miss caused by the cache being too small","a miss in the TLB"],"answer":0,"explain":"A compulsory miss is the first access to a block.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Conflict misses are reduced by","options":["a bigger clock","larger blocks only","a deeper pipeline","higher associativity"],"answer":3,"explain":"Conflict misses are reduced by higher associativity.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Average memory access time is","options":["miss rate × miss penalty","hit time + miss rate × miss penalty","hit time × miss rate","hit time + miss penalty"],"answer":1,"explain":"AMAT = hit time + miss rate × miss penalty.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A write-back cache writes a block to the next level","options":["on every store","only when the program ends","when a dirty block is replaced","never"],"answer":2,"explain":"A write-back cache writes a block to the next level when a dirty block is replaced.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A 32 KB 4-way set-associative cache with 64-byte blocks has how many sets?","options":["128","256","512","64"],"answer":0,"explain":"Step 1: sets = capacity/(block size × ways). Step 2: 32,768/(64 × 4) = 32,768/256 = 128.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In MESI, the E state means the line is","options":["dirty and shared","clean and the only cached copy","invalid","dirty and exclusive"],"answer":1,"explain":"E (exclusive) means the line is clean and the only cached copy.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"False sharing occurs when","options":["two cores read the same variable","a lock is held too long","two threads use the same variable without synchronisation","different variables written by different cores lie in the same cache line"],"answer":3,"explain":"False sharing: different variables written by different cores lie in the same cache line, so the line ping-pongs although no variable is shared.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A GPU warp is","options":["a group of 32 threads executing in lock-step","a cache line","a memory bank","a block of 1024 threads"],"answer":0,"explain":"A warp is a group of 32 threads executing in lock-step.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Branch divergence in a warp causes","options":["faster execution","cache misses","the paths to execute one after the other (serialisation)","a deadlock"],"answer":2,"explain":"Divergence in a warp makes the two paths execute one after the other (serialisation).","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In the roofline model attainable performance is","options":["peak compute only","the minimum of peak compute and bandwidth × arithmetic intensity","bandwidth only","the maximum of the two"],"answer":1,"explain":"Roofline: attainable performance = min(peak compute, bandwidth × arithmetic intensity).","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which factor does a better compiler primarily change?","options":["clock rate","instruction count","cache size","pipeline depth"],"answer":1,"explain":"A better compiler mainly changes the instruction count (IC) of the program.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Two machines both have a 2000 MIPS rating. Then","options":["they run every program equally fast","they may still differ in execution time on the same program","both have CPI 1","both have the same clock rate"],"answer":1,"explain":"MIPS = f/(CPI × 10⁶) ignores how many instructions are needed, so equal MIPS can still mean different execution times.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Dynamic power in CMOS is proportional to","options":["V × f","V^2 × f","V^3","f only"],"answer":1,"explain":"CMOS dynamic power P = αCV²f.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A load-use hazard in the classic five-stage pipeline with forwarding costs","options":["0 cycles","1 cycle","2 cycles","3 cycles"],"answer":1,"explain":"With forwarding a load-use hazard costs one stall cycle, because the data exists only at the end of MEM.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A branch target buffer (BTB) stores","options":["the data of recent loads","the target addresses of recent branches","the register values","the page table"],"answer":1,"explain":"A branch target buffer stores the target addresses of recently taken branches.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The 2-bit saturating counter predictor changes its prediction only after","options":["one wrong outcome","two consecutive wrong outcomes","four wrong outcomes","it never changes"],"answer":1,"explain":"A 2-bit saturating counter changes its prediction only after two consecutive wrong outcomes.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In Tomasulo's algorithm results are broadcast on","options":["the memory bus","the program counter","the common data bus","the TLB"],"answer":2,"explain":"In Tomasulo's algorithm results are broadcast on the common data bus (CDB).","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"WAW dependences are removed by","options":["forwarding","register renaming","bigger caches","a faster clock"],"answer":1,"explain":"WAW (and WAR) name dependences are removed by register renaming.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A VLIW processor relies on","options":["the hardware to find parallelism","the compiler to schedule independent operations","the operating system","speculative threads"],"answer":1,"explain":"A VLIW processor relies on the compiler to schedule independent operations.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which statement about the reorder buffer is true?","options":["it speeds up cache misses","instructions commit from it in program order","it holds only loads","it replaces the TLB"],"answer":1,"explain":"The reorder buffer lets instructions complete out of order but commit in program order.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A 64 KB direct-mapped cache with 32-byte blocks (32-bit addresses) has an index of","options":["10 bits","11 bits","12 bits","13 bits"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1: sets = 65,536/(32 × 1) = 2048. Step 2: index = log₂ 2048 = 11 bits (offset 5 bits, tag 32 - 16 = 16 bits).","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A fully associative cache has no","options":["capacity misses","conflict misses","compulsory misses","hits"],"answer":1,"explain":"A fully associative cache has no conflict misses (any block can go in any line).","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Increasing the block size generally","options":["always lowers the miss rate","reduces compulsory misses but may raise the miss penalty and conflict misses","removes capacity misses","lowers the hit time"],"answer":1,"explain":"Larger blocks reduce compulsory misses (spatial locality) but increase the miss penalty and may increase conflict misses.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Write-through with a write buffer is used to","options":["avoid stalling the processor on stores","avoid cache coherence","speed up loads","reduce tag bits"],"answer":0,"explain":"Write-through with a write buffer lets the processor continue without stalling on stores.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In MESI a write to a line in the S state","options":["is silent","requires invalidating the other copies","leaves the line S","fetches the line from disk"],"answer":1,"explain":"A write to a line in the S state must first invalidate the other copies.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Under sequential consistency","options":["writes can be reordered freely","all processors see one global order that respects each program's order","caches are not allowed","only one core runs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sequential consistency: all processors see one global order that respects each program's order.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"On x86, which reordering can be observed (total store order)?","options":["load-load","store-store","a store followed by a load to a different address","none"],"answer":2,"explain":"Under x86 total store order a store can be delayed past a later load to a different address.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"On a GPU, memory accesses are fastest when","options":["the threads of a warp access adjacent addresses (coalesced)","every thread accesses a random address","all threads write the same address","data is in the disk cache"],"answer":0,"explain":"GPU memory accesses are fastest when the threads of a warp access adjacent addresses (coalescing).","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"An algorithm with arithmetic intensity below the ridge point of the roofline is","options":["compute bound","memory bound","impossible","latency bound only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Below the ridge point the memory system limits performance: the kernel is memory bound.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For a given task, lowering both voltage and frequency by the same factor generally","options":["reduces power and energy","increases power","leaves energy unchanged","increases energy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Step 1: P ∝ V²f; scaling both by the same factor gives P ∝ s³. Step 2: the task takes 1/s times as long, so energy ∝ s², which is lower.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
